# Description: INTERNAL AUTHENTICATE - asks the card to prove it holds a secret key.
# Category: templates
#
# WHAT THIS DOES
# INTERNAL AUTHENTICATE sends 8 bytes of challenge data to the card; the
# card encrypts or MACs them with a secret key stored inside itself and
# returns the 8-byte result (the cryptogram). Only a card holding the right
# key can produce the answer your system expects, so this proves the card
# is genuine. The command itself does not modify the card, but it only
# works when the card actually contains a key for the referenced key
# number.
#
# !!! EDIT BEFORE USE !!!
# The challenge bytes below (00 01 02 03 04 05 06 07) are a PLACEHOLDER.
# For a real authentication, replace them with fresh unpredictable bytes -
# replaying an old challenge weakens the proof - and make sure P2 selects
# the key reference your card expects.
#
# THE COMMAND, BYTE BY BYTE
#   00                         class byte: standard interindustry commands
#   88                         instruction: INTERNAL AUTHENTICATE
#   00                         P1 = 0 (reserved)
#   00                         P2: key reference 0 (PLACEHOLDER)
#   08                         Lc: 8 challenge bytes follow
#   00 01 02 03 04 05 06 07    PLACEHOLDER challenge data
#
# WHAT YOU SHOULD SEE
#   8 bytes of response data (the cryptogram) followed by SW 90 00, or
#   SW 69 88 / 69 85 if the referenced key does not exist or its conditions
#   of use are not satisfied.
00 88 00 00 08 00 01 02 03 04 05 06 07
